{
  id: "dictator", set: "roman-republic", act: "Caesar", title: "Dictator Perpetuo", era: "46 – 44 BC", minutes: 9,
  intro: "Caesar won the war completely. Then he had to decide what to be, and there was no Roman answer.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Finishing it",
      text: ["The Pompeian cause outlived Pompey. At **Thapsus** in North Africa in 46 BC Caesar destroyed the last major senatorial army. **Cato the Younger**, besieged at Utica, arranged his friends' escape, read Plato on the immortality of the soul, and killed himself rather than accept a pardon.",
        "It was a deliberate final move against Caesar's greatest weapon: clemency only works on people willing to be forgiven. At **Munda** in Spain in 45 BC, against Pompey's sons, Caesar reportedly said he had often fought for victory but that day fought for his life. He won. The war was over."],
      key: "Caesar won at Thapsus and Munda; Cato killed himself at Utica rather than accept a pardon." },
    { title: "What he actually did with power",
      text: ["The reform programme was genuinely impressive. He replaced Rome's chaotic lunar calendar with the **Julian calendar**, 365¼ days, which — with one later adjustment — is the one on your wall. He expanded the Senate to about 900, admitting Italians and provincials who had never had a route in.",
        "He settled tens of thousands of veterans and urban poor in colonies from Corinth to Carthage, restructured debt without the general cancellation his radical supporters wanted, capped the grain dole, and launched enormous public works. Much of it was overdue, competent and popular."],
      key: "Caesar reformed the calendar, expanded the Senate, settled veterans in colonies and restructured debt." },
    { title: "The crown problem",
      text: ["The forms went wrong. Statues, a gilded chair, his image on coins while alive, honours voted in flattering excess. Tradition says he stayed seated when the Senate approached him in a body. In February 44 BC he was made ***dictator perpetuo*** — dictator for life, the emergency office made permanent.",
        "At the Lupercalia, **Mark Antony** publicly offered him a diadem and Caesar refused it, twice, to loud applause. Whether that was a genuine refusal or a staged test of opinion is **Contested** and always will be. What matters is that Rome now had a man with permanent supreme power who did not need the title, and a political class raised on the belief that killing kings is the highest form of patriotism."],
      key: "Made dictator for life in 44 BC, Caesar held permanent supreme power; his intentions toward kingship remain contested." },
    { title: "The Ides of March",
      text: ["Around sixty senators joined the conspiracy. The public face was **Marcus Junius Brutus** — descended, he believed, from the Brutus who expelled the kings, and a nephew of Cato — persuaded by his brother-in-law **Cassius** that his ancestry obliged him. Caesar had pardoned both after Pharsalus and promoted them since.",
        "On 15 March 44 BC they stabbed him in the portico where the Senate was meeting, twenty-three times. He died at the foot of a statue of **Pompey**. The reported last words to Brutus, *kai su, teknon* — you too, child? — are **Traditional / Legendary**; Suetonius himself notes that most sources say he said nothing.",
        "The conspirators had a plan for the murder and none for the morning after. They expected the Republic to resume on its own. Instead they had removed the only man capable of controlling the armies, and left his lieutenant and his heir alive."],
      key: "Caesar was assassinated on 15 March 44 BC by around 60 senators led by Brutus and Cassius, with no plan for afterwards." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why did Cato kill himself at Utica?", options: ["He was condemned to death", "To deny Caesar the political victory of pardoning him", "He was mortally wounded", "He had been proscribed"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Clemency only works on people who accept it; Cato's suicide was a final refusal to be forgiven by a tyrant." },
    { q: "How is Caesar's refusal of the diadem at the Lupercalia classified?", options: ["Established as a genuine refusal", "Established as a staged test", "Contested — his intention cannot be determined", "Traditional / Legendary — it never happened"], correct: 2,
      explain: "The event is well attested; what it meant is a genuine and permanent historical disagreement." },
    { q: "What was the conspirators' central failure?", options: ["They failed to kill Caesar", "They had no plan for what followed the assassination", "They were betrayed before acting", "They acted too early in his career"], correct: 1,
      explain: "They assumed the Republic would restart by itself, and left Antony and Caesar's heir in play." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "antony-octavian", set: "roman-republic", act: "The End", title: "The Heir Nobody Expected", era: "44 – 42 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "An eighteen-year-old with a famous name inherits a political machine, and everyone underestimates him.",
  beats: [
    { title: "The funeral",
      text: ["**Mark Antony**, Caesar's co-consul and most trusted lieutenant, moved first. He secured Caesar's papers and treasury from his widow, negotiated an amnesty that left the conspirators' offices intact — and then delivered the **funeral oration**.",
        "He read out the will, which left money to every Roman citizen and Caesar's gardens to the public, and displayed the bloodied body and toga. The crowd rioted, burned Caesar's body in the Forum and hunted the conspirators through the streets. Brutus and Cassius left Rome. Antony had won the city without a battle."],
      key: "Antony's funeral oration and the reading of Caesar's will turned Rome against the conspirators." },
    { title: "The name in the will",
      text: ["The will also named an heir: **Gaius Octavius**, eighteen, Caesar's great-nephew, adopted posthumously as his son. He was studying in Illyria. Everyone advised him to refuse; he went straight to Italy, took the name **Caesar**, and started paying the legacy to citizens out of his own pocket.",
        "Antony treated him as a boy and refused to hand over the funds. Octavian raised private troops from Caesar's veterans on the strength of the name alone. **Cicero** — who despised Antony — backed him publicly, and privately remarked that the young man should be praised, honoured and got rid of. Octavian heard about it."],
      key: "Octavian, 18, accepted Caesar's name and inheritance, raised veterans privately, and was underestimated by everyone." },
    { title: "The Second Triumvirate",
      text: ["After a confused year of shifting alliances, Antony, Octavian and Caesar's cavalry commander **Lepidus** met on an island near Bononia in 43 BC and did what the first three had done — except this time they made it **law**, a five-year commission to reconstitute the state.",
        "They funded and secured it with **proscriptions**, on Sulla's model: published death lists, confiscated estates, bounties. Around 300 senators and 2,000 equestrians were condemned. Each triumvir gave up friends and relatives to the others' lists.",
        "**Cicero** was on Antony's. He was caught fleeing and killed; his head and the hands that wrote the *Philippics* against Antony were nailed to the Rostra in the Forum where he had spoken."],
      key: "The Second Triumvirate was legally established in 43 BC and funded by proscriptions; Cicero was among the killed." },
    { title: "Philippi",
      text: ["In 42 BC the triumvirs met Brutus and Cassius at **Philippi** in Macedonia. The battle was fought in two stages, confused and badly coordinated. Cassius, believing wrongly that Brutus had also been beaten, killed himself. Brutus won his half of the field, lost the second engagement three weeks later, and killed himself too.",
        "The Republican cause died with them. What remained was three men splitting the Roman world: Antony took the wealthy East, Octavian the difficult job of Italy and the veterans, Lepidus was shuffled off to Africa and gradually out of the story."],
      key: "At Philippi (42 BC) Brutus and Cassius died and the Republican cause ended; Antony took the East, Octavian Italy." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "How did Antony turn Rome against the conspirators?", options: ["A military coup", "A funeral oration, Caesar's will and the displayed body", "A Senate vote", "By exiling them legally"], correct: 1,
      explain: "He converted grief into a riot without needing a single soldier." },
    { q: "How did the Second Triumvirate differ from the First?", options: ["It had four members", "It was a legally established commission, not a private pact", "It excluded military command", "It was approved by Brutus"], correct: 1,
      explain: "Formalising it in law made its proscriptions and confiscations legally enforceable." },
    { q: "What was Octavian's principal asset in 44 BC?", options: ["A large personal fortune", "Command of Caesar's legions", "The name 'Caesar' and the loyalty it carried with veterans", "Senate backing"], correct: 2,
      explain: "He had no office and no army — the name was the machine, and he used it faster than anyone anticipated." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "actium", set: "roman-republic", act: "The End", title: "Actium", era: "41 – 30 BC", minutes: 8,
  intro: "Two men, one world, and a propaganda war that decided the naval one before it was fought.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Tarsus",
      text: ["In 41 BC **Antony** summoned **Cleopatra** to Tarsus to answer for Egypt's conduct during the civil wars. She arrived on a gilded barge dressed as **Aphrodite** — an entrance Plutarch describes at length and Shakespeare later borrowed wholesale.",
        "The alliance that followed was practical on both sides. Antony needed Egyptian grain and money for a Parthian campaign; Cleopatra needed a Roman protector for her throne and her son. It was also, by every account, personal. She bore him twins, and later a third child."],
      key: "Antony and Cleopatra allied at Tarsus in 41 BC — Egyptian money for Roman protection, and a personal bond." },
    { title: "Two very different decades",
      text: ["Antony spent the 30s BC in the East, where his major **Parthian campaign** in 36 BC was an expensive failure that cost tens of thousands of men.",
        "Octavian spent them in Italy doing unglamorous, decisive work: settling veterans, clearing **Sextus Pompey** from the seas with his admiral **Agrippa** to secure the grain supply, sidelining Lepidus, and building a reputation as the man who restored order. He also relentlessly worked Roman opinion. Antony was married to Octavian's sister **Octavia** for much of this period, which made his open life with Cleopatra useful ammunition."],
      key: "Antony's eastern campaigns failed while Octavian secured Italy, the grain supply and Roman public opinion." },
    { title: "The Donations, and a will",
      text: ["In 34 BC Antony staged the **Donations of Alexandria**: enthroned with Cleopatra, he distributed Roman-claimed eastern territories to their children and confirmed **Caesarion** as Caesar's true son — a direct challenge to Octavian's entire claim.",
        "Octavian's response was masterful and probably illegal. He seized Antony's **will** from the Vestal Virgins and read it publicly: Antony wanted burial in Alexandria, and confirmed the Donations. Whether the document was authentic or doctored is **Contested**. It did the job. Rome declared war — carefully, on **Cleopatra**, a foreign queen, so that Octavian was defending Rome rather than fighting a civil war."],
      key: "The Donations of Alexandria and Antony's leaked will let Octavian declare war on Cleopatra rather than on a Roman." },
    { title: "31 BC",
      text: ["At **Actium** in September 31 BC, Antony's fleet was blockaded, disease-ridden and losing deserters. In the battle Cleopatra's squadron broke out through a gap and made for Egypt; Antony followed her with a portion of the fleet, abandoning the rest, which surrendered. His land army gave up within a week.",
        "The following year Octavian took Alexandria. Antony killed himself, reportedly on a false report of Cleopatra's death. Cleopatra died shortly after, at 39, rather than be paraded in a Roman triumph — the asp is **Traditional / Legendary**, and poison is equally plausible.",
        "**Caesarion**, aged seventeen, was hunted down and killed; his existence was intolerable to a man whose claim rested on being Caesar's son. Egypt, the last great Hellenistic kingdom, became Roman property."],
      key: "Actium (31 BC) and the fall of Alexandria ended Antony and Cleopatra; Egypt became Roman and Caesarion was killed." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "Why did Rome declare war on Cleopatra rather than Antony?", options: ["Antony had already died", "It framed the conflict as defending Rome from a foreign queen, not a civil war", "Antony was legally immune", "Cleopatra had attacked Italy"], correct: 1,
      explain: "The framing was pure political engineering: no Roman had to be seen fighting a Roman." },
    { q: "How is Cleopatra's death by asp classified?", options: ["Established", "Probable", "Traditional / Legendary", "Contested between ancient authors only"], correct: 2,
      explain: "It is the famous version but cannot be established; poison is at least as plausible on the evidence." },
    { q: "Why was Caesarion killed?", options: ["He led a revolt", "He was Egyptian royalty by law", "A rival claim to be Caesar's son was intolerable to Octavian", "He died in the fighting at Actium"], correct: 2,
      explain: "Octavian's entire legitimacy rested on being Caesar's heir; a biological son was a permanent threat." },
  ],
},
{
  id: "augustus", set: "roman-republic", act: "The End", title: "The Republic That Never Ended", era: "27 BC", minutes: 7,
  intro: "Octavian's genius was not winning. It was working out how to keep power by publicly giving it back.",
  beats: [
    { title: "Handing it all back",
      text: ["In January 27 BC, Octavian came before the Senate and formally **resigned his extraordinary powers**, returning the state to the Senate and People of Rome. His own account in the ***Res Gestae*** puts it exactly that way.",
        "The Senate, packed with his supporters and acutely aware that he controlled every legion that mattered, begged him to take responsibility back. He accepted a large provincial command — the provinces with the armies in them — and the honorific name **Augustus**, meaning revered or consecrated. He called himself ***princeps***, first citizen, a Republican word for an unRepublican position."],
      key: "In 27 BC Octavian resigned his powers, was handed back the armed provinces, and took the name Augustus." },
    { title: "Why the trick worked",
      text: ["Every Republican form survived. The Senate met. Consuls were elected annually. Assemblies voted. Augustus held offices that already existed, notably **tribunician power** — the veto and sacrosanctity of a tribune, without the office — and superior *imperium* over the provinces.",
        "Nothing looked new. But the armies, the treasury of the armed provinces, Egypt as personal property, and the appointment of the men who mattered all ran through one household. Romans who would have died fighting a king accepted a first citizen. He had learned the precise lesson of the Ides of March: take the power, refuse the title."],
      key: "Augustus kept every Republican form intact while controlling the armies, the money and the appointments." },
    { title: "So when did the Republic end?",
      text: ["There is no agreed date, and the disagreement is real rather than trivial. Candidates: **133 BC**, when political murder began; **49 BC**, when Caesar crossed the Rubicon; **44 BC**, the dictatorship for life; **31 BC**, Actium; **27 BC**, the settlement.",
        "This app classifies the end of the Republic as **Interpretation** rather than Established, because the answer depends on what you think the Republic essentially *was*. If it was a set of institutions, they outlasted Augustus. If it was a political culture in which no one man could dominate, it died a long time before he did."],
      key: "The Republic's end date is an interpretation, not a fact: 133, 49, 44, 31 and 27 BC are all defensible." },
  ],
  check: [
    { q: "What did Augustus receive in exchange for resigning his powers in 27 BC?", options: ["A lifetime consulship", "Command of the provinces containing the armies, plus the name Augustus", "The dictatorship", "The office of Pontifex Maximus only"], correct: 1,
      explain: "He gave up titles and kept the legions — the substance of power dressed as its renunciation." },
    { q: "Why does this app classify the end of the Republic as 'Interpretation'?", options: ["The sources are lost", "No ancient writer discusses it", "The answer depends on what you think the Republic essentially was", "Modern historians agree on 44 BC"], correct: 2,
      explain: "It is a definitional argument, not a gap in the evidence — which is a different kind of uncertainty." },
  ],
}
